From b2777b72287e7ce88b5ade63e273b93a1ef4edfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Iaroslav Voitovych <yaroslav.voytovych@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 05:29:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Bluetooth: hci_conn: don't reuse an aborting SCO
 connection

hci_connect_sco() finds an existing connection to the destination by
type and address only, and sends a Setup Synchronous Connection only if
that connection is BT_OPEN or BT_CLOSED. A connection that is being
disconnected is neither: hci_abort_conn() sets abort_reason and queues
the Disconnect but leaves the state alone, so the connection stays
BT_CONNECTED until the Disconnection Complete event arrives.

A socket that connects to the same device in that window is therefore
handed the dying connection. Before commit e6720779ae61 ("Bluetooth:
SCO: Use kref to track lifetime of sco_conn") the closed socket stayed
attached to the connection in BT_DISCONN until that event, so
sco_chan_add() failed such a connect() with EBUSY. Since then the
closed socket is detached at once and the new socket takes its place:
no Setup Synchronous Connection is sent, sco_connect() reports the
socket connected at once, and the Disconnection Complete then closes
it with ECONNABORTED. Closing a SCO socket and connecting a new one
right away, as PipeWire does during an HFP codec change, ends with a
socket that reported success and never had a link.

Fail the connect with -ECONNABORTED when the connection found has
abort_reason set, and drop the ACL hold hci_connect_acl() took. The
caller learns at once, with the error the socket would otherwise get
later, and can connect again once the link is gone.

hci_connect_sco() runs under hdev->lock, but hci_conn_timeout() sets
abort_reason through hci_abort_conn() without it, so mark the new read
and that write with READ_ONCE() and WRITE_ONCE().

Fixes: e6720779ae61 ("Bluetooth: SCO: Use kref to track lifetime of sco_conn")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Signed-off-by: Iaroslav Voitovych <yaroslav.voytovych@gmail.com>
---
 net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c
index 603ddb7fe3d0d..69b7943ddedd7 100644
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c
@@ -1909,6 +1909,14 @@ struct hci_conn *hci_connect_sco(struct hci_dev *hdev, int type, bdaddr_t *dst,
 			hci_conn_drop(acl);
 			return sco;
 		}
+	} else if (READ_ONCE(sco->abort_reason)) {
+		/* The connection is being disconnected and stays BT_CONNECTED
+		 * until the Disconnection Complete event, so no setup would
+		 * be sent for it and it would be closed under the new socket
+		 * as soon as the event arrives.
+		 */
+		hci_conn_drop(acl);
+		return ERR_PTR(-ECONNABORTED);
 	}
 
 	if (sco->link) {
@@ -3272,7 +3280,7 @@ int hci_abort_conn(struct hci_conn *conn, u8 reason)
 
 	bt_dev_dbg(hdev, "handle 0x%2.2x reason 0x%2.2x", conn->handle, reason);
 
-	conn->abort_reason = reason;
+	WRITE_ONCE(conn->abort_reason, reason);
 
 	/* Cancel the connect attempt. A return of 0 means the create command
 	 * was still queued and got dequeued, so there is nothing to disconnect.
-- 
